I would simply change the code from:
int defaultSnooze = parseInt(editSnooze.getText().toString());
to:
int defaultSnooze;
defaultSnoozeString = editSnooze.getText().toString();
if(defaultSnoozeString != null) {
   defaultSnooze = parseInt(defaultSnoozeString);
} else {
   defaultSnooze = SnoozeActivity.getDefaultSnooze(above);
}

Since this is only the default of how the dialog will start I guess that any solution will work...

Although it could be caused by the String itself being null, if it is an invalid integer for some other reason then the nullpointer exception will come from the conversion of `Integer` to `int` which occurs on the referenced line. 

How about something very similar to that:

```
int defaultSnooze;
try {
      defaultSnooze = parseInt(editSnooze.getText().toString());
    } catch (NullPointerException e) {
      defaultSnooze = SnoozeActivity.getDefaultSnooze(above);
    }
```

Yes I think the above is pretty safe but could also be done like:

```
Integer defaultSnooze = parseInt(editSnooze.getText().toString());
if (defaultSnooze == null)
                 defaultSnooze = SnoozeActivity.getDefaultSnooze(above);
```

I wrote it as above with the catch because then it doesn't change the type of `defaultSnooze` and there are a couple of other places which might need the same treatment.

Regardless of the implementation I think I've got an answer to the main question which is what to do when its null.
